#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""pool top: your two Macs side by side, live: CPU per core, memory, what every Claude session costs, and which
sessions to move where. The two Macs and their labels come from ~/.config/pool/pool.json; one "travels" with you and
one "stays" put (optionally running an always-on service).

  pool top                 the monitor in this terminal (? lists the keys; the mouse works too)
  pool top --once          one text snapshot of both Macs, the sessions and the suggested moves
  pool top --window        open a Ghostty window running the monitor
  pool top --agent         JSON lines for PoolBar, the menu bar widget: both Macs, the suggestions, and the popup
                           to show on this Mac when it's the one you're using
  pool top --stream [s]    this Mac's samples as JSON lines every s seconds (the other Mac reads them over ssh)
  pool top --move <session> <tab|moved|bg> <host>   move a session (what the m key and PoolBar's Move run)
  pool top --close <item>...                        close idle sessions or leftover processes (PoolBar's Close)
  pool top --snooze <session> <minutes>             no popup for that session for a while

Tiers (`pool tier`, or t here): home (travel) sessions belong on the Mac that goes home with you; stay sessions are
the ones that matter least and belong on the always-on Mac; float sessions go wherever there's room. Suggestions
follow from that: home work on the stay Mac moves to the travel Mac; stay work on the travel Mac moves to the stay Mac
when it has room; float work leaves a Mac that has been past its ceiling for a minute, or is short of memory, for the
other; CPU-heavy float work prefers the faster Mac's cores and idle float sessions holding memory prefer the Mac with
more free RAM. The stay Mac's ceiling is a little lower so its always-on service keeps room to burst. Once either Mac
passes 65% of capacity, the two should stay within 35 points of each other: float work moves from the busier one.

Things nobody is using are offered for closing: Claude sessions untouched for a day (their conversations are kept),
and processes left running after the Claude session that started them ended (dev servers, test browsers, MCP servers,
Bash-tool shells). Never the always-on service, Claude Code's own background machinery (which it needs to start a
session), or any pinned folder. If the always-on service is down for two minutes, you're asked to restart it.

Capacity is counted in the stay Mac's performance cores; a core on the faster Mac is worth more (the per-core weights
are in the config), so the same percentage on the faster Mac is more real work.
"""
